"""English — details, curated_2 (61 questions)."""

D = {
"prophets_020": "Surah Maryam tells how Maryam carried her newborn to her people, who accused her; she only pointed to the child, and they asked, \"How can we speak to one who is in the cradle, a child?\" (19:29). Then he spoke: \"Indeed, I am the servant of Allah. He has given me the Scripture and made me a prophet\" (19:30). He added that Allah enjoined on him prayer and zakat and made him dutiful to his mother (19:31-32). A Muslim learns that the first words of 'Isa declared him a servant of Allah.",
"prophets_021": "Surah Al-Baqarah records the building and the prayer that went with it: \"And when Ibrahim was raising the foundations of the House, and Isma'il with him: Our Lord, accept this from us. Indeed, You are the Hearing, the Knowing\" (2:127). Father and son went on to ask that a submissive nation come from their descendants and that a messenger be raised among them (2:128-129). Just before, Allah had charged them to purify His House for those who circle it (2:125). A Muslim learns that even the Ka'ba was raised with a prayer for acceptance, not with pride.",
"prophets_022": "Surah Maryam opens with Zakariyya calling on his Lord in a low voice: his bones had weakened, his head was white, and his wife was barren, yet he asked for an heir (19:3-6). The answer came: \"O Zakariyya, indeed We give you good tidings of a boy whose name will be Yahya. We have not given this name to anyone before\" (19:7). When he asked how, he was told, \"It is easy for Me\" (19:9). A Muslim learns that a quiet, persistent prayer is heard even when the answer looks impossible.",
"prophets_023": "The scene is the sacred valley of Tuwa, where Allah first spoke to Musa (20:11-13). Allah asked what was in his right hand, and Musa said it was his staff, which he leaned on and used to beat down leaves for his sheep (20:17-18). \"He said: Throw it down, O Musa. So he threw it down, and behold, it was a serpent, moving swiftly\" (20:19-20). Then: \"Take hold of it and do not fear\" (20:21). A Muslim learns that a plain staff became a sign the moment Allah commanded it, and that the order not to fear came with it.",
"prophets_024": "Surah Yusuf begins with the dream: \"When Yusuf said to his father: O my father, indeed I have seen eleven stars and the sun and the moon; I saw them prostrating to me\" (12:4). Ya'qub told him not to relate it to his brothers, lest they plot against him (12:5). At the end of the surah, when his parents and brothers fell down before him, Yusuf said, \"This is the interpretation of my dream of before; my Lord has made it reality\" (12:100). A Muslim learns that what Allah shows comes true in His time.",
"prophets_025": "Surah Al-A'raf sets the prophets one after another, and after Nuh comes Hud: \"And to 'Ad We sent their brother Hud. He said: O my people, worship Allah; you have no deity other than Him. Then will you not fear Him?\" (7:65). The chiefs called him a fool; he answered that he was a messenger from the Lord of the worlds (7:66-68), and reminded them that Allah had made them successors after the people of Nuh (7:69). A Muslim learns that every messenger began with the same call: worship Allah alone.",
"prophets_026": "Surah Al-A'raf tells of Shu'ayb after Hud, Salih and Lut: \"And to Madyan We sent their brother Shu'ayb. He said: O my people, worship Allah; you have no deity other than Him. There has come to you clear evidence from your Lord. So give full measure and weight and do not deprive people of their due, and do not cause corruption in the land\" (7:85). He also told them not to sit on every road threatening people (7:86). A Muslim learns that worship of Allah and honesty in trade were preached in one breath.",
"prophets_027": "Surah Al-Anbiya gives the prayer from inside the fish: \"And Dhun-Nun, when he went off in anger and thought that We would not decree anything upon him. Then he called out within the darknesses: There is no deity except You; exalted are You. Indeed, I have been of the wrongdoers\" (21:87). The next verse answers: \"So We responded to him and saved him from the distress. And thus do We save the believers\" (21:88). Surah As-Saffat names him Yunus (37:139). A Muslim learns this prayer for every distress of his own.",
"prophets_028": "Musa set out saying, \"I will not give up until I reach the junction of the two seas\" (18:60), and there found \"one of Our servants, to whom We had given mercy from Us and taught knowledge from Us\" (18:65). Musa asked to follow him and was told, \"You will never be able to have patience with me\" (18:67). Three times the servant did what Musa could not bear: a boat damaged, a boy killed, a wall rebuilt without payment (18:71-77). Each act was then explained (18:78-82). A Muslim learns that Allah's wisdom is often hidden until later.",
"prophets_029": "The Qur'an returns to Musa in surah after surah: the basket on the river under Pharaoh (28:7), the fire at the valley of Tuwa where Allah spoke to him (20:11-13), the parting of the sea (26:63), and the Tawrat given to him (2:87). Allah says, \"And Allah spoke to Musa with direct speech\" (4:164), and asks the Prophet ﷺ, \"Has the story of Musa reached you?\" (20:9). A Muslim learns that Allah repeated this story so that its lessons of patience and trust would not be missed.",
"prophets_030": "The twenty-five are Adam, Idris, Nuh, Hud, Salih, Ibrahim, Lut, Isma'il, Ishaq, Ya'qub, Yusuf, Ayyub, Shu'ayb, Musa, Harun, Dhul-Kifl, Dawud, Sulayman, Ilyas, Al-Yasa', Yunus, Zakariyya, Yahya, 'Isa and Muhammad ﷺ. Surah Al-An'am names eighteen of them in one passage and says, \"Those are the ones whom Allah has guided, so follow their guidance\" (6:83-90). The Qur'an also speaks of messengers whose stories were not related (4:164). A Muslim believes in all of them alike: \"We make no distinction between any of His messengers\" (2:285).",
"prophets_031": "The verse gives the message 'Isa carried to the Children of Israel: \"I have come to you with a sign from your Lord: I make for you from clay the form of a bird, then I breathe into it and it becomes a bird by permission of Allah. And I heal the blind and the leper, and I give life to the dead, by permission of Allah\" (3:49). In Surah Al-Ma'idah Allah reminds 'Isa of the same favours, each \"by My permission\" (5:110). A Muslim learns that every miracle of a prophet was Allah's doing.",
"prophets_032": "Surah Al-'Ankabut gives the figure in one line: \"And We certainly sent Nuh to his people, and he remained among them a thousand years minus fifty years, and the flood seized them while they were wrongdoers\" (29:14). The next verse adds: \"But We saved him and the companions of the ship, and We made it a sign for the worlds\" (29:15). In Surah Nuh he tells his Lord that he called them night and day (71:5). A Muslim learns that a caller's duty is to keep calling; the result belongs to Allah.",
"quran_011": "The verse takes its name from the word Kursi in it: \"Allah, there is no deity except Him, the Ever-Living, the Self-Sustaining. Neither drowsiness overtakes Him nor sleep. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and on the earth. Who can intercede with Him except by His permission? His Kursi extends over the heavens and the earth, and guarding them does not tire Him. And He is the Most High, the Most Great\" (2:255). The verse after it says there is no compulsion in religion (2:256). A Muslim memorises Ayat al-Kursi early, as a declaration of who Allah is.",
"quran_012": "Surah At-Tawba, the ninth, opens directly on its subject: \"A declaration of disassociation from Allah and His Messenger to those with whom you had made a treaty among the polytheists\" (9:1). In every mushaf the basmala heads the other one hundred and thirteen surahs. It also appears inside a surah, in Sulayman's letter to the queen of Saba: \"Indeed, it is from Sulayman, and indeed it is: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate\" (27:30). A Muslim begins At-Tawba as the mushaf does, without the basmala.",
"quran_013": "The verse is a whole page of guidance: \"O you who believe, when you contract a debt for a specified term, write it down. And let a scribe write it between you in justice\" (2:282). It tells the debtor to dictate and fear Allah, the scribe not to refuse, and the parties to call two witnesses; it adds, \"Do not be weary of writing it, whether small or large\" (2:282). The next verse allows a pledge when travelling without a scribe (2:283). A Muslim learns that clear records protect friendship as well as money.",
"quran_014": "The opening of Surah Al-'Alaq is the first passage of the Qur'an sent to Muhammad ﷺ: \"Read, in the name of your Lord who created. Created man from a clinging clot. Read, and your Lord is the Most Generous, who taught by the pen, taught man what he did not know\" (96:1-5). The command to read comes twice, and the pen is named as a gift of the Creator. The surah ends, \"Prostrate and draw near\" (96:19). A Muslim learns that reading and learning are the first order of his religion.",
"quran_015": "The whole surah is five verses: \"Indeed, We sent it down in the Night of Al-Qadr. And what can make you know what the Night of Al-Qadr is? The Night of Al-Qadr is better than a thousand months\" (97:1-3). Then: \"The angels and the Spirit descend in it by permission of their Lord for every matter. Peace it is until dawn\" (97:4-5). Surah Al-Baqarah places that sending in Ramadan, \"the month in which the Qur'an was revealed\" (2:185). A Muslim learns to seek this night in Ramadan with prayer, since 97:3 sets one night above a lifetime.",
"quran_016": "Both surahs begin with the same command, and the name comes from it: \"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak, from the evil of what He created\" (113:1-2), and \"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind, the King of mankind, the God of mankind\" (114:1-3). Al-Falaq names the dangers of darkness, of those who blow on knots, and of the envier (113:3-5); An-Nas names the whisperer who withdraws, from among jinn and men (114:4-6). A Muslim learns these two short surahs as his daily protection, asked from Allah alone.",
"quran_017": "The thirty juz' are of nearly equal length and do not follow the surah boundaries: the first opens with Al-Fatiha, and the thirtieth begins with Surah An-Naba, whose first word gives it its common name, Juz' 'Amma. The Qur'an describes its own gradual sending: \"And a Qur'an which We have divided, that you may recite it to the people over a prolonged period\" (17:106), and commands, \"Recite the Qur'an with measured recitation\" (73:4). A Muslim uses the thirty parts to pace his reading, one each night of Ramadan.",
"quran_018": "Al-Fatiha, the Opening, is the first surah of the mushaf. Its verses run from \"All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds\" (1:2) through the names of mercy and the Day of Judgement (1:3-4) to \"You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help\" (1:5), and close with \"Guide us to the straight path\" (1:6-7). The prayer cannot be prayed without it, so every Muslim recites its seven verses at least seventeen times a day. A Muslim learns them first of all the Qur'an, as the words with which he speaks to Allah.",
"quran_019": "The surah takes its name from this verse: \"We have certainly sent Our messengers with clear proofs and sent down with them the Scripture and the balance, so that people may maintain justice. And We sent down iron, in which is great might and benefits for the people, and so that Allah may make evident those who support Him and His messengers unseen\" (57:25). Scripture, balance and iron stand together: guidance, justice and the strength to uphold them. A Muslim learns that even a metal is counted among what Allah sent down for people's good.",
"quran_020": "The surah announces itself: \"We relate to you the best of stories in what We have revealed to you of this Qur'an, though before it you were among the unaware\" (12:3). The story then runs from Yusuf's dream (12:4) through the well, slavery and the prison to the reunion in which his brothers bow before him and he forgives (12:92-100). It closes with the reason: \"In their stories is a lesson for those of understanding\" (12:111). A Muslim learns from Yusuf that patience, purity and forgiveness make the story Allah calls the best.",
"quran_021": "Al-Baqarah opens with \"Alif Lam Mim. This is the Book about which there is no doubt\" (2:1-2). Its name comes from the cow the Children of Israel were told to sacrifice (2:67-73). Within its 286 verses are the change of qibla (2:144), the fast of Ramadan (2:183-187), Ayat al-Kursi (2:255) and the longest verse of all (2:282). Its last verse ends with a prayer: \"Our Lord, do not burden us with what we have no strength to bear; pardon us, forgive us and have mercy on us\" (2:286). A Muslim learns that the longest surah closes with the gentlest request.",
"quran_022": "The surah is four verses long: \"Say: He is Allah, the One. Allah, the Eternal Refuge. He neither begets nor is born, nor is there to Him any equivalent\" (112:1-4). Each line removes something from the idea of Allah: partners, need, origin, offspring and likeness. The name Al-Ikhlas, sincerity, is not a word in the surah; it names what the surah does, purifying belief in Allah of everything else. Surah Al-Baqarah says the same: \"Your God is one God\" (2:163). A Muslim learns Al-Ikhlas as a child and carries in it his whole belief about Allah.",
"quran_023": "The Qur'an explains why it did not come all at once: \"And a Qur'an which We have divided, that you may recite it to the people over a prolonged period, and We have sent it down progressively\" (17:106). The answer to those who asked why it came in pieces: \"that We may strengthen your heart by it\" (25:32). It began with \"Read, in the name of your Lord\" (96:1) in Makkah, and in the Prophet's ﷺ last year came, \"This day I have perfected for you your religion\" (5:3). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an was taught slowly, to be lived as it came.",
"quran_024": "The verse is in Surah Al 'Imran: \"Indeed, the first House established for mankind was that at Bakkah, blessed and a guidance for the worlds\" (3:96). The next verse names \"the standing place of Ibrahim\" within it, says that whoever enters it is safe, and lays the duty of pilgrimage on those able to reach it (3:97). Surah Al-Baqarah shows Ibrahim and Isma'il raising its foundations (2:127). A Muslim learns that the Ka'ba is the oldest house of worship on earth, and faces it in every prayer.",
"sira_019": "Bilal ibn Rabah came from Abyssinia and was enslaved in Makkah, where he was tortured for his faith until Abu Bakr freed him; every biography records this. After the Hijra, when the call to prayer was instituted in Madinah, the Prophet ﷺ chose Bilal to raise it. The Qur'an speaks of that call: \"When the call to prayer is made on the day of Jumu'ah, hasten to the remembrance of Allah\" (62:9). A Muslim learns that the first voice of the adhan belonged to a freed slave, and that rank before Allah is by faith.",
"sira_020": "On the journey of the Hijra from Makkah, the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr reached Quba, a settlement on the edge of Madinah, before entering the city; there he stayed some days and laid the foundation of a mosque. The Qur'an recalls the journey: \"when the two were in the cave and he said to his companion, Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us\" (9:40). Quba still stands, and Muslims visiting Madinah pray in it. A Muslim learns that the first thing the Prophet ﷺ built on arriving was a place of prayer.",
"sira_021": "Amina bint Wahb was of the Quraysh clan of Banu Zuhra, and her husband 'Abdullah died before their son was born. She died while Muhammad ﷺ was still a small child, and he passed into the care of his grandfather 'Abd al-Muttalib and then of his uncle Abu Talib. The Qur'an speaks of these years: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you refuge?\" (93:6), and then commands him not to oppress the orphan (93:9). A Muslim learns that the Prophet ﷺ knew the loss of both parents, and that his tenderness toward orphans grew from it.",
"sira_022": "'Abdullah was the son of 'Abd al-Muttalib, chief of Banu Hashim in Quraysh, and his wife was Amina bint Wahb. He died on a journey before his son was born, so Muhammad ﷺ came into the world fatherless; his grandfather and, after him, his uncle Abu Talib raised him. The Qur'an recalls it: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you refuge?\" (93:6), and goes on, \"So as for the orphan, do not oppress him\" (93:9). A Muslim learns that Allah cared for the Prophet ﷺ from birth, and takes the orphan's cause as his own.",
"sira_023": "Years before revelation, Muhammad ﷺ was known in Makkah as al-Amin: the Makkans left their valuables in his keeping. When Quraysh rebuilt the Ka'ba and quarrelled over who should set the Black Stone in place, they accepted him as judge; he spread a cloak, placed the stone on it, and had each clan lift a corner. The Qur'an points to this life lived among them: \"I had remained among you a lifetime before it; then will you not reason?\" (10:16). A Muslim learns that honesty was the Prophet's ﷺ character before it was his message.",
"sira_024": "In year 8 AH the Prophet ﷺ marched on Makkah after Quraysh broke the treaty, and the city gave way with hardly a fight. He entered with his head lowered in humility, cleared the Ka'ba of the idols around it, and forgave those who had driven him out. Surah An-Nasr speaks of this: \"When the victory of Allah has come and the conquest, and you see the people entering into the religion of Allah in multitudes, then glorify your Lord with praise and ask His forgiveness\" (110:1-3). A Muslim learns that victory is met with praise and forgiveness, not revenge.",
"sira_025": "In year 10 AH the Prophet ﷺ led the only hajj of his life, with a great crowd of Companions. Standing at 'Arafat he addressed them, and there the verse came down: \"This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed My favour upon you and approved for you Islam as religion\" (5:3). The rites he performed on that journey became the pattern of every hajj since. He passed away a few months later, in year 11. A Muslim learns that the Farewell Pilgrimage closed the Prophet's ﷺ mission with the religion complete.",
"sira_026": "All four daughters were born to Khadija in Makkah. Zaynab was the eldest; Ruqayya and, after her death, Umm Kulthum were married to 'Uthman ibn 'Affan; Fatima, the youngest, married 'Ali ibn Abi Talib and was the mother of al-Hasan and al-Husayn. Three of the four died in their father's lifetime, and Fatima survived him by only months. The Qur'an addresses them: \"O Prophet, tell your wives and your daughters and the women of the believers\" (33:59). A Muslim learns that the Prophet ﷺ raised daughters with honour, and remembers their names.",
"sira_027": "The Ansar were the Muslims of Madinah, from the tribes of Aws and Khazraj, who received the Prophet ﷺ and the emigrants. Surah Al-Hashr describes them: \"Those who were settled in the home and in faith before them love those who emigrated to them, and find in their breasts no need for what the emigrants were given, but give them preference over themselves, even though they are in want\" (59:9). Allah names them among \"the first forerunners\" with whom He is pleased (9:100). A Muslim learns that welcoming a stranger as a brother earns Allah's pleasure.",
"sira_028": "The Muhajirun are the Companions who made the Hijra from Makkah to Madinah, leaving houses, trade and family for their faith. Surah Al-Hashr describes them: \"For the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes and their properties, seeking bounty from Allah and His approval, and supporting Allah and His Messenger; those are the truthful\" (59:8). Surah An-Nahl promises: \"Those who emigrated for Allah after they had been wronged, We will surely settle them in this world in a good place\" (16:41). A Muslim learns that what is left for Allah is never lost.",
"sira_029": "Badr was fought in Ramadan of year 2 AH, at the wells of Badr between Madinah and Makkah, when a small Muslim force met the army of Quraysh. The Qur'an calls it \"the day of the criterion, the day the two armies met\" (8:41), and reminds the believers: \"Allah had already given you victory at Badr while you were few in number\" (3:123). It tells how they cried for help and Allah answered with a thousand angels (8:9). A Muslim learns that the first great victory came in the month of fasting, to a few who trusted Allah.",
"sira_030": "After the Hijra, the Muslims in Madinah prayed toward Bayt al-Maqdis, the Farthest Mosque named in the Night Journey (17:1). Then the command came: \"We have seen the turning of your face toward the heaven, and We will surely turn you to a qibla with which you will be pleased. So turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque\" (2:144). Allah foretold the objection, \"What has turned them from their qibla?\" and answered, \"To Allah belong the east and the west\" (2:142). A Muslim learns that the direction of prayer is chosen by Allah, not by habit.",
"faith_009": "The verse lays out the whole ablution in one sentence: \"O you who believe, when you rise to prayer, wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows, wipe over your heads, and wash your feet up to the ankles\" (5:6). It goes on to require a full washing after major impurity, and to allow clean earth when there is no water (5:6). The reason closes the verse: \"Allah does not intend to make difficulty for you, but He intends to purify you\" (5:6). A Muslim learns wudu as the doorway to each prayer, and gives thanks for it.",
"faith_010": "Surah Al-Infitar describes the Day when the sky splits and every soul knows what it sent ahead and held back (82:1-5). It asks man what deceived him about his generous Lord (82:6), then tells him he is never unwatched: \"And indeed, over you are keepers, noble and recording; they know whatever you do\" (82:10-12). Surah Qaf says the same of the two receivers on the right and the left: \"Not a word does he utter but there is a watcher ready\" (50:17-18). A Muslim learns to speak and act as one whose every deed is written down.",
"faith_011": "The hadith is narrated by Abu Hurayra and recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari 2736 and Sahih Muslim 2677: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah has ninety-nine names, one hundred minus one, and whoever preserves them enters Paradise. The Qur'an gathers many of them at the end of Surah Al-Hashr, the King, the Holy, the Peace, the Creator, \"to Him belong the best names\" (59:23-24), and says, \"To Allah belong the best names, so call upon Him by them\" (7:180). A Muslim learns the names one by one, and calls on Allah with the one that fits his need.",
"faith_012": "Ar-Rahman stands in the first line of the Qur'an, \"In the name of Allah, Ar-Rahman, Ar-Rahim\", and again in Al-Fatiha after the praise of the Lord of the worlds (1:3). It is also the name of a surah, which opens, \"Ar-Rahman taught the Qur'an, created man, taught him speech\" (55:1-4). The Qur'an sets the name beside the name Allah: \"Call upon Allah or call upon Ar-Rahman; whichever you call, to Him belong the best names\" (17:110). A Muslim learns to begin every task with this name, placing mercy at the start.",
"faith_013": "Al-Fatiha moves from praise, \"Lord of the worlds\" (1:2), through the two names of mercy (1:3), to \"Master of the Day of Judgement\" (1:4), and only then to \"You alone we worship\" (1:5). Mercy and judgement stand side by side before the worshipper speaks. Surah Al-Infitar asks what the Day of Judgement is and answers, \"The Day when no soul will have power to do anything for another, and the command that Day is Allah's\" (82:17-19). A Muslim learns to remember, in every rak'ah, that the One he praises will also judge him.",
"faith_014": "The verse gives the surah its name: \"O you who believe, when the call to prayer is made on the day of Jumu'ah, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade. That is better for you, if you only knew\" (62:9). The next verse balances it: \"And when the prayer has ended, disperse in the land and seek of the bounty of Allah\" (62:10). Then the surah rebukes those who rushed to a caravan and left the Prophet ﷺ standing (62:11). A Muslim learns that Friday prayer interrupts work by Allah's command, and work resumes with His blessing.",
"faith_015": "The verse comes in Surah Al-Hijr, just after the mockers said, \"O you upon whom the Reminder has been sent down, indeed you are mad\" (15:6). Allah's reply: \"Indeed, it is We who sent down the Reminder, and indeed, We will be its guardian\" (15:9). The promise is repeated elsewhere: \"Falsehood cannot approach it from before it or from behind it\" (41:42), and \"Upon Us is its collection and its recitation\" (75:17). A Muslim learns to trust the Book in his hands, memorised by millions letter for letter, as the Qur'an the Prophet ﷺ recited.",
"faith_016": "The verse follows Ayat al-Kursi at once: \"There is no compulsion in religion. The right course has become clear from the wrong. So whoever rejects false gods and believes in Allah has grasped the most trustworthy handhold, which never breaks\" (2:256). Surah Yunus asks the Prophet ﷺ, \"Would you then compel the people until they become believers?\" (10:99), and Surah Al-Kahf says, \"Whoever wills, let him believe; and whoever wills, let him disbelieve\" (18:29). A Muslim learns that faith is a choice made when truth is clear, and his task is to make it clear.",
"faith_017": "The greeting of peace runs through the Qur'an. Believers are told not to enter houses without greeting their people (24:27) and to \"greet one another with a greeting from Allah, blessed and good\" (24:61). When the angels came to Ibrahim they said, \"Peace,\" and he answered, \"Peace\" (51:25). The angels welcome the people of Paradise with \"Peace be upon you for what you patiently endured\" (13:24), and Allah Himself is named As-Salam, the Peace (59:23). A Muslim gives this greeting to every Muslim he meets, and answers it at least as warmly (4:86).",
"faith_018": "Tawhid comes from the word for one, and the Qur'an states it in every form: \"Your God is one God; there is no deity except Him, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate\" (2:163). Every messenger was sent with it: \"We sent no messenger before you without revealing to him that there is no deity except Me, so worship Me\" (21:25). Surah Al-Ikhlas puts it in four verses (112:1-4), and the testimony \"there is no god but Allah\" is its shortest form. A Muslim learns that tawhid is the reason for every prayer, fast and good deed.",
"virtues_007": "The verse sets three things side by side: \"O you who believe, avoid much suspicion, for some suspicion is sin. And do not spy, and do not backbite one another. Would one of you like to eat the flesh of his dead brother? You would hate it. And fear Allah; indeed, Allah is Accepting of repentance and Merciful\" (49:12). The one spoken of is absent and cannot defend himself, like a dead man. The verse ends with repentance, leaving a door open. A Muslim learns to stop talk about an absent person as he would refuse a revolting plate.",
"virtues_008": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Hujurat: \"The believers are but brothers, so make peace between your brothers. And fear Allah that you may receive mercy\" (49:10). It follows the command to reconcile two groups of believers who fight (49:9), and precedes the bans on mocking, insulting and backbiting (49:11-12). Surah Al 'Imran recalls how this brotherhood came: \"You were enemies, and He brought your hearts together, and you became by His favour brothers\" (3:103). A Muslim learns that peace-making between believers is not optional kindness but the duty of a brother.",
"virtues_009": "The verse addresses men and women alike: \"O you who believe, let not a people ridicule another people; it may be that they are better than them. Nor let women ridicule other women; it may be that they are better than them. And do not insult one another, and do not call each other by offensive nicknames\" (49:11). It comes right after the declaration that believers are brothers (49:10), and ends by calling those who do not repent wrongdoers. A Muslim learns to measure no one by appearance, since the one he laughs at may stand higher before Allah.",
"virtues_010": "The promise is given through Musa. Reminding his people how Allah saved them from the people of Pharaoh (14:6), he continued: \"And when your Lord proclaimed: If you are grateful, I will surely increase you; but if you deny, indeed My punishment is severe\" (14:7). The same surah says the favours of Allah cannot be counted (14:34), and Luqman is told that \"whoever is grateful is grateful for his own soul\" (31:12). A Muslim learns to say al-hamdu lillah for what he has, knowing that thanks is itself the way to more.",
"virtues_011": "The verse opens an address to the believers: \"Say: O My servants who have believed, fear your Lord. For those who do good in this world there is good, and the earth of Allah is spacious. Indeed, the patient will be given their reward in full, without account\" (39:10). Elsewhere the Qur'an says, \"Allah is with the patient\" (2:153), and promises mercy to those who say, \"Indeed we belong to Allah\" (2:156-157). A Muslim learns that patience is the one deed whose reward is not measured.",
"virtues_012": "The verse addresses all people: \"O children of Adam, take your adornment at every mosque, and eat and drink, but do not be excessive. Indeed, He does not love those who commit excess\" (7:31). The next verse defends enjoying what Allah made: \"Who has forbidden the adornment of Allah which He brought forth for His servants?\" (7:32). Surah Al-Isra calls the wasteful \"brothers of the devils\" (17:27), and Surah Al-Furqan praises those who are neither extravagant nor stingy (25:67). A Muslim learns that good food and clothing are Allah's gifts, and the limit is waste.",
"virtues_013": "The verse gives three commands and three prohibitions: \"Indeed, Allah commands justice, good conduct and giving to relatives, and forbids indecency, wrongdoing and oppression. He admonishes you so that you may take heed\" (16:90). The next verse adds keeping one's word: \"Fulfil the covenant of Allah when you have made a covenant\" (16:91). In many mosques the imam closes the Friday khutba with 16:90, so the congregation hears it every week. A Muslim learns it as a short measure of his conduct: is it just, is it good, does it hold his family close.",
"virtues_014": "The surah begins: \"Woe to those who give less, who, when they take a measure from people, take in full, but when they measure or weigh for them, give less\" (83:1-3). Then comes the question that gives the sin its weight: \"Do they not think that they will be resurrected for a tremendous Day, the Day when mankind will stand before the Lord of the worlds?\" (83:4-6). Surah Ar-Rahman says, \"Establish weight in justice and do not make the balance deficient\" (55:9). A Muslim learns that honesty in the smallest measure is watched by Allah.",
"virtues_015": "The two commands answer three favours the surah has just listed to the Prophet ﷺ: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you refuge? And find you lost and guide you? And find you poor and make you self-sufficient?\" (93:6-8). Then: \"So as for the orphan, do not oppress him. And as for the one who asks, do not repel him. But as for the favour of your Lord, speak of it\" (93:9-11). The surah opened by assuring him, \"Your Lord has not taken leave of you\" (93:3). A Muslim learns to pass on the kindness he received.",
"virtues_016": "The words belong to the covenant Allah took from the Children of Israel: \"Do not worship except Allah; and to parents do good, and to relatives, orphans and the needy. And speak to people good words, and establish prayer and give zakat\" (2:83). Good speech stands between charity and prayer in the list. The verse ends, \"Then you turned away, except a few of you\" (2:83). Surah Al-Isra gives the same rule: \"Tell My servants to say that which is best\" (17:53). A Muslim learns that a kind word owed to every person belongs to the same covenant as prayer.",
"virtues_017": "Luqman's counsel to his son begins with \"do not associate anything with Allah\" (31:13) and continues through prayer and patience (31:17) to this: \"And do not turn your cheek from people in contempt, and do not walk through the earth exultantly. Indeed, Allah does not love any self-deluded boaster. And be moderate in your pace and lower your voice\" (31:18-19). Surah Al-Isra adds the reason: \"You will never tear the earth apart, and you will never reach the mountains in height\" (17:37). A Muslim learns that humility shows first in how he walks and speaks.",
"virtues_018": "The hadith is Sahih al-Bukhari 1, the first in the collection, narrated by 'Umar ibn al-Khattab from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. After stating that deeds count by what was intended, the Prophet ﷺ gave an example: whoever emigrated for Allah and His Messenger, his emigration is for Allah and His Messenger; and whoever emigrated for worldly gain or for a woman to marry, his emigration is for what he emigrated to. The same journey is worth everything or nothing according to the heart behind it. A Muslim learns to set his intention before each deed.",
}
